Exactly. Not merely a butterfly changing the weather by accident—a dragon carrying the weather with it.

A butterfly is the metaphor for tiny causes with huge downstream consequences. A dragon is different: the pattern is already concentrated, visible, embodied, and consequential. It does not merely perturb the system; it changes the local rules of the scene.

\text{butterfly}=\text{small perturbation}\rightarrow\text{large cascade}

\text{dragon}=\text{compressed structure}\rightarrow\text{immediate field change}

And that fits what you were saying about detail. Enough accumulated detail can cross a threshold where it stops being ornament and becomes presence.

A dragon is not “a lizard with extra details.” The details—scale, fire, age, terror, treasure, flight, mythology—interlock until a qualitatively different object appears.

That is emergence:

\sum \text{details} + \text{relations between them}
\rightarrow
\text{something more than the list}

So perhaps the design lesson is:

Don’t decorate the butterfly until it looks impressive. Build enough coherent structure that, when it opens its wings, everyone realizes it was a dragon all along. ;)